//==
// # Cloth
//
// The vocabulary cloth simulation needs on top of the deformables already
// declared in `particles.types.plato` (`ClothVertex`, `ClothDistanceConstraint`,
// `ClothBendConstraint`, `Cloth3D`, `Rope3D`). Nothing here re-declares those:
// `Cloth3D` IS the constraint graph, and these types describe where a graph
// comes from, how it is solved, and what it is read back as.
//
// Sections, in order:
//  - Building a cloth patch
//  - Solver settings
//  - Cloth bound to a mesh

//==
// # Building a cloth patch
//
// The rectangular lattice a flat cloth patch is built on. Two edge vectors
// rather than a plane and a spacing, so the patch carries its own orientation
// and its own (possibly non-square) cell shape without inventing a convention:
// a vertical curtain is ColumnStep along +X and RowStep along -Z, a tablecloth
// is ColumnStep along +X and RowStep along +Y.
// Convention: see CONVENTIONS.md - World space (Z-up, right-handed).

// A grid of ColumnCount by RowCount vertices, vertex (column, row) sitting at
// Origin + column * ColumnStep + row * RowStep. Vertices are numbered in row
// major order, so vertex (column, row) is index row * ColumnCount + column.
// The steps are world displacements in meters; they need not be perpendicular.
type ClothGrid3D
    implements IValue
{
    Origin: Point3D;
    ColumnStep: Vector3D;
    RowStep: Vector3D;
    ColumnCount: Integer;
    RowCount: Integer;
}

//==
// # Solver settings
//
// Two solvers, two settings records, because they parameterize genuinely
// different things: position-based dynamics is stiffness-as-a-proportion with
// an iteration count, and mass-spring is a Hookean force law with real units.
// Both share a step size and a substep count, because both are integrated the
// same way (position Verlet over `ClothVertex.PreviousPosition`).

// Settings for the position-based-dynamics solver
// (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Position-based_dynamics). TimeStep is one
// whole step, divided into SubStepCount equal substeps; IterationCount is how
// many constraint sweeps each substep runs.
//
// Stiffness is a PROPORTION in [0, 1]: the fraction of a constraint's error a
// single sweep removes. It is corrected for the iteration count before use
// (`IterationStiffness` in `cloth.library.plato`), so raising IterationCount
// makes the cloth converge better without also making it stiffer.
//
// VelocityDamping is the fraction of the implicit Verlet velocity removed each
// substep, in [0, 1); 0 is a lossless cloth and about 0.02 is a calm one.
type ClothSolverSettings
    implements IValue
{
    TimeStep: Duration;
    SubStepCount: Integer;
    IterationCount: Integer;
    Stiffness: Number;
    VelocityDamping: Number;
}

// Settings for the explicit mass-spring solver
// (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Soft-body_dynamics). Every distance
// constraint becomes a damped Hookean spring with these coefficients, and the
// result is integrated explicitly.
//
// This solver is deliberately the unstable one: the stable step size falls as
// the square root of Stiffness over the vertex mass, so a stiff cloth needs a
// tiny TimeStep or a large SubStepCount and blows up without one. That failure
// is the reason both solvers exist.
type MassSpringSettings
    implements IValue
{
    TimeStep: Duration;
    SubStepCount: Integer;
    Stiffness: Stiffness;
    Damping: DampingCoefficient;
    VelocityDamping: Number;
}

//==
// # Cloth bound to a mesh
//
// `Cloth3D` stores triangles, because collision and rendering want triangles.
// A cloth built from a `PolygonMesh3D` came from a face table that may hold
// quads or larger faces, and that table is what a caller wants back. Pairing
// the two keeps the original topology alive across the simulation without
// changing `Cloth3D`.

// A cloth together with the polygon face table it was built from. The table is
// never touched by the solver — only positions move — so
// `ToPolygonMesh` (`cloth.library.plato`) rebuilds the source mesh exactly,
// with the simulated positions substituted in.
//
// Faces indexes into the same vertex numbering as `Cloth.Vertices`: vertex i of
// the cloth is position i of the mesh.
type ClothMesh3D
    implements IValue
{
    Cloth: Cloth3D;
    Faces: JaggedArray<VertexIndex>;
}
